# This file is a safety device, not housekeeping.
#
# soundstage is a PUBLIC repository. ADR-0002 (docs/adr/0002-the-repo-is-public-and-holds-no-content.md)
# records that content never enters it: the material this toolkit is pointed at is not
# ours to publish, and it is gitignored from the first commit, before there is anything
# to leak. ADR-0005 records that the OpenAI key lives in .env and nowhere else.
#
# Read both ADRs before loosening anything here.
#
# The extension rules are the net that needs no layout: nothing in them depends on a
# folder name, so they hold however the repo is arranged. Extensions are written as
# bracket classes because gitignore matching is case-sensitive on Linux, and cameras and
# exporters happily produce .MP4 and .PDF.
#
# Extensions alone are not enough. Content arrives in whatever file type it arrives in -
# a narration script is a .txt, a plan is a .md - and no list of extensions will catch
# those without also catching the repo's own writing. So the two directories content
# actually lands in are ignored whole, by name, and that is the rule ADR-0002 leans on.

# Secrets - the OpenAI key lives here and nowhere else (ADR-0005)
.env
.env.*

# Source documents (ADR-0002)
*.[dD][oO][cC]
*.[dD][oO][cC][xX]
*.[pP][dD][fF]
*.[pP][pP][tT][xX]

# Content directories - ignored whole, whatever the file type (ADR-0002)
#
# Source material goes in /inputs/, everything made from it goes in /outputs/. Both are
# derived from or are material that is not ours to publish, so neither is committable
# here regardless of extension. Leading slashes anchor these to the repo root: they are
# these two directories, not any folder anywhere that happens to share the name.
/inputs/
/outputs/

# Rendered video and generated audio (ADR-0002)
*.[mM][pP]4
*.[mM][oO][vV]
*.[wW][eE][bB][mM]
*.[wW][aA][vV]
*.[mM][pP]3
*.[mM]4[aA]
